findings.finding:
Association only, not causal. Runs with a flagged victim show:
- more drop_swept, both in total and per Bayes-delivered step;
- more selections, giveups and fallback steps;
- fewer LO-continuation steps, because they stay longer in Bayes mode, which confounds the comparison.
Strategy steering does not collapse at high DEAD: while an undetected victim remains, the share of airborne searcher steps steered by targeting is higher when DEAD > 0.5. The issue rate per searcher-step is lower, except in vs3r. Code check on the swept rule (the lead's correction is right): measure() divides uncovered p and dead by the same z<1, which RAISES Sp at an uncovered held target. burn_over() moves mass only on burning cells and never renormalises. predict() conserves mass. A held target's Sp therefore falls only through local loss (coverage of its disc, burn-over on burning cells inside it, motion outflow), never through global DEAD growth. The report's section 15 (i) is wrong as worded. The higher drop_swept rate in flagged runs fits local depletion of a thin residual, but the recorded data cannot separate the channels.

